window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-275a09b4f8630225"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"codex-environments\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"codex\">Codex 运行环境</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 后追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该文档的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中，打开 ChatGPT 下拉菜单并选择 <strong>Codex</strong>。开始 Codex 对话时，可选择任务运行的位置：</p> <ul> <li><strong>Local（本地）</strong>：直接在当前项目目录中工作。</li> <li><strong>Worktree（工作树）</strong>：在 Git worktree 中隔离修改。<a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306\">了解更多</a>。</li> <li><strong>Cloud（云端）</strong>：根据已发布、可重复使用的环境，在独立工作区中启动远程任务。如何准备和发布任务所需的仓库、依赖项及工具，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">云环境</a>。</li> </ul> <p><strong>Local（本地）</strong>和 <strong>Worktree（工作树）</strong>对话都在你的计算机上运行。使用 <strong>Work in（工作位置）</strong>选择 <strong>This computer（此计算机）</strong>或 <strong>Cloud（云端）</strong>。对于本地 Git 项目，单独的 <strong>Worktree（工作树）</strong>控制项可以让你把修改与当前检出目录隔离。</p> <p>云端任务需要你有权访问一个已发布的环境。如果没有可用环境，可以在网页版或桌面应用中创建一个，或者向工作区管理员申请访问共享环境。选择环境与创建或发布环境是两件不同的事。获得共享访问权限后，你可以使用该环境，但不会因此获得编辑它的权限。</p> <p>你也可以在网页端或移动端启动和继续云端任务。在移动端，打开 <strong>Codex</strong>，然后选择一个已发布的环境。</p> <p>要创建环境，请在网页版或桌面应用中新建任务。选择 <strong>Work in（工作位置）</strong> &gt; <strong>Cloud（云端）</strong>，打开 <strong>Select environment（选择环境）</strong>，再选择 <strong>Create environment（创建环境）</strong>。Codex 会检查你选定的仓库，并帮助准备和测试环境，然后你可以发布它。</p> <p>每个 Cloud 任务都有自己的工作文件。任务中的文件变更不会更新可重复使用的环境。若要更改后续任务的起点，请更新并重新发布环境配置。</p> <p>云端任务只能使用其云环境中提供的文件和服务。你计算机上的本地文件、正在运行的进程、浏览器登录状态和 VPN 访问不会自动传到云端任务。</p> <p>更多术语请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-0310706ee8617b64\">Codex 概念</a>。</p> <blockquote> <p>插图：桌面端输入框中打开了 Work in 菜单，其中显示 This computer 和 Cloud；Worktree 是另一个单独的选项</p> </blockquote>","en":"<h1 id=\"codex-environments\">Codex environments</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>In the ChatGPT desktop app, open the ChatGPT dropdown and select <strong>Codex</strong>. When starting a Codex chat, choose where it runs:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Local</strong>: work directly in your current project directory.</li> <li><strong>Worktree</strong>: isolate changes in a Git worktree. <a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306\">Learn more</a>.</li> <li><strong>Cloud</strong>: start a remote task in its own workspace from a published, reusable environment. See <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">Cloud environments</a> to prepare and publish the repositories, dependencies, and tools your tasks need.</li> </ul> <p>Both <strong>Local</strong> and <strong>Worktree</strong> chats run on your computer. Use <strong>Work in</strong> to choose <strong>This computer</strong> or <strong>Cloud</strong>. For a local Git project, the separate <strong>Worktree</strong> control lets you isolate changes from your current checkout.</p> <p>A cloud task requires a published environment you can access. If none is available, create one on the web or in the desktop app, or ask a workspace admin for access to a shared environment. Selecting an environment is separate from creating or publishing it. Shared access lets you use the environment; it doesn't grant permission to edit it.</p> <p>You can also start and continue cloud tasks on the web or on mobile. On mobile, open <strong>Codex</strong> and select a published environment.</p> <p>To create an environment, start a new task on the web or in the desktop app. Choose <strong>Work in</strong> &gt; <strong>Cloud</strong>, open <strong>Select environment</strong>, then select <strong>Create environment</strong>. Codex inspects your selected repositories and helps prepare and test their setup before you publish it.</p> <p>Each Cloud task has its own working files. File changes in a task don't update the reusable environment. To change the starting point for future tasks, update and republish the environment's setup.</p> <p>A cloud task uses the files and services available in its cloud environment. Your computer's local files, running processes, browser sign-ins, and VPN access aren't automatically transferred to it.</p> <p>For more terms, see <a href=\"#/article/a-0310706ee8617b64\">Codex concepts</a>.</p> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: Desktop composer with the Work in menu open to This computer and Cloud, and a separate Worktree option</p> </blockquote>"};
